---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const demo = "https://ossa.abalazade.workers.dev";
---

<Base
  title="OSSA: a small-batch storefront"
  description="A front-end demo of what a limited clothing drop needs past the homepage: stock per variant, a cart that respects it, and no fake checkout."
  path="/work/ossa"
>
  <a class="back" href="/">Index</a>

  <h1 class="lede">OSSA</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">
    A made-up clothing brand, built to show what a limited drop needs past the homepage. Nothing
    here takes payment.
  </p>

  <p><a class="action" href={demo}>Open the demo</a></p>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Why it's built this way</h2>
    <p>
      Most store demos track stock per product. Real drops sell out per variant: the black tee in XS
      is gone while the L is still there. Almost everything interesting on a product page follows
      from that.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>No framework.</strong> Six products and one cart don't need a bundler or a dependency
      tree to keep patched. Three files load instantly.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>No real checkout.</strong> On a hosted platform, checkout belongs to the platform and
      is its most tested part. Replacing it is how stores lose money, so the button is a stub.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Clothes are SVG, not photos.</strong> No stock images to license, and picking a colour
      recolours the garment, which is the variant-to-image link a real catalogue needs.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Stock is a default plus exceptions.</strong> Instead of ninety hand-written rows,
      <code>{"{ default: 9, overrides: { 'Black / XS': 0 } }"}</code> says the same thing.
    </p>

    <h2>What it does</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Six products, one of them fully sold out.</li>
      <li>Colour and size pickers with live stock, a size guide and fabric details.</li>
      <li>Unavailable options stay visible, crossed out.</li>
      <li>A cart drawer capped at real stock, with free-shipping progress, that survives a reload.</li>
      <li>Back-in-stock signup on sold-out variants, and a newsletter form.</li>
      <li>Real product URLs that you can share, and a back button that works.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Base>
